Below are the columns that look important for answering  
“Which repository with an approved license in `licenses.md` had the highest combined total of forks, issues, and watches in April 2022?”

• GITHUB_REPOS_DATE.GITHUB_REPOS.LICENSES  
  – column "repo_name":  will be used to join with the events table.  
  – column "license":     the license string, so we can keep only repos whose license is on the *approved* list from `licenses.md`.

• GITHUB_REPOS_DATE.YEAR._2022  
  – column "type":        tells us the kind of GitHub event (“ForkEvent”, “IssuesEvent”, “WatchEvent”, etc.).  
  – column "created_at":  a numeric timestamp that lets us keep only events that happened in April 2022.  
  – column "repo":        VARIANT that contains the repository object; the attribute "name" inside it is the repo’s full name (we will cast it to STRING).  

Typical conditions that will probably be needed  
1) "license" IN (<approved‑list>)                                       -- keep only approved licenses  
2) "type"  IN ('ForkEvent','IssuesEvent','WatchEvent')                  -- events we want to count  
3) "created_at" BETWEEN <start_of_April_2022> AND <end_of_April_2022>   -- restrict to April 2022  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that gradually walk from very simple inspection to the final analytic query.  
Every identifier is enclosed in double quotes as requested.

```sql
-- 1) Description:  Look at a few rows of the LICENSES table
SELECT *
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LICENSES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description:  See the distinct license strings that occur
SELECT DISTINCT "license"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LICENSES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description:  Show sample repos that have the MIT license (as a sanity check)
SELECT "repo_name", "license"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LICENSES"
WHERE  "license" ILIKE '%mit%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description:  Inspect a handful of rows from the 2022 events table
SELECT *
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description:  Pull the repo name and event type out of the JSON to be sure the path works
SELECT 
       t."type",
       t."repo":VARIANT:"name"::STRING         AS "repo_name",
       t."created_at"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022" t
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description:  Take only events that look like forks, issues, or watches
SELECT 
       t."type",
       t."repo":VARIANT:"name"::STRING AS "repo_name",
       t."created_at"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022" t
WHERE  t."type" IN ('ForkEvent','IssuesEvent','WatchEvent')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description:  Rough attempt to get events that happened in April 2022 (placeholders for the exact epoch numbers)
SELECT 
       t."type",
       t."repo":VARIANT:"name"::STRING AS "repo_name",
       t."created_at"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022" t
WHERE  t."type" IN ('ForkEvent','IssuesEvent','WatchEvent')
  AND  t."created_at" BETWEEN 1648771200 AND 1651363199   -- 2022‑04‑01 .. 2022‑04‑30 (seconds‑since‑epoch placeholder)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description:  Count how many forks, issues, and watches each repo received in April 2022
SELECT 
       t."repo":VARIANT:"name"::STRING AS "repo_name",
       COUNT(*)                       AS "april_2022_event_count"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022" t
WHERE  t."type" IN ('ForkEvent','IssuesEvent','WatchEvent')
  AND  t."created_at" BETWEEN 1648771200 AND 1651363199
GROUP BY 1
ORDER BY 2 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description:  Combine the LICENSES table to keep only repos with an approved license (example uses MIT & Apache‑2.0 as placeholders)
SELECT 
       e."repo_name",
       SUM(e."event_cnt") AS "total_april_2022_events"
FROM (
      SELECT 
             t."repo":VARIANT:"name"::STRING AS "repo_name",
             COUNT(*)                       AS "event_cnt"
      FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022" t
      WHERE  t."type" IN ('ForkEvent','IssuesEvent','WatchEvent')
        AND  t."created_at" BETWEEN 1648771200 AND 1651363199
      GROUP BY 1
     ) e
JOIN "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LICENSES" l
  ON e."repo_name" = l."repo_name"
WHERE l."license" IN ('mit','apache-2.0')     -- replace with full list from licenses.md
GROUP BY 1
ORDER BY 2 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description:  Final query – which single repository (approved license only) had the highest combined total of forks, issues, and watches in April 2022
SELECT 
       e."repo_name",
       SUM(e."event_cnt") AS "total_april_2022_events"
FROM (
      SELECT 
             t."repo":VARIANT:"name"::STRING AS "repo_name",
             COUNT(*)                       AS "event_cnt"
      FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022" t
      WHERE  t."type" IN ('ForkEvent','IssuesEvent','WatchEvent')
        AND  t."created_at" BETWEEN 1648771200 AND 1651363199
      GROUP BY 1
     ) e
JOIN "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LICENSES" l
  ON e."repo_name" = l."repo_name"
WHERE l."license" IN ('mit','apache-2.0', 'gpl-3.0', 'bsd-3-clause', 'epl-2.0')  -- expand to full approved list
GROUP BY 1
ORDER BY 2 DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

These ten progressively more complex queries should help you explore the relevant columns and ultimately identify the repository that tops the combined total in April 2022.
